{#-
Cursor idiom macros for the plugin template collection.

Chunk: docs/chunks/dualplugin_cursor_scaffold - Cursor half of the flavor-substitution interface

This file is the Cursor half of the interface whose Claude half is
partials/claude/idioms.md.jinja2. Both implement the same five signatures,
so a command template imports one or the other and never knows which:

  frontmatter(name, description, allowed_tools=None)
  agent_frontmatter(name, description, tools)
  generated_marker(source_template)
  probe(label, command)
  probe_intro()                  -- framing for an ad-hoc probe block
  canonical_preamble()           -- task guidance via {% call %} body
  plugin_root()
  arguments()                    -- where the operator's request lands
  arguments_ref()                -- inline prose reference to that request
  arguments_token(placeholder)   -- the request embedded in a command

All macros emit content WITHOUT a trailing newline; the calling template owns
line structure.

THE ONE IDIOM THAT REALLY DIFFERS: context probes. Claude Code preprocesses
`` !`command` `` in a skill body — the harness runs the command and splices
its output in before the model reads the file. Cursor has no equivalent, so
the same syntax would reach the model as literal text and every probe would
silently report nothing. The Cursor macros keep the commands identical and
move the execution to the agent: the preamble instructs it to run the probes
first and read the results as its Context block. Same commands, same
fallback chains, same interpretation guidance -- different actor.
-#}
{%- macro frontmatter(name, description, allowed_tools=None) -%}
{#- allowed_tools is accepted and ignored: `allowed-tools` is Claude-only
    frontmatter, and Cursor's skill format is name + description. The
    parameter stays in the signature because call sites are shared between
    flavors. -#}
---
name: {{ name }}
description: {{ description }}
---
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- Chunk: docs/chunks/dualplugin_cursor_render - Agent (subagent) frontmatter.
    tools is accepted and ignored: Cursor's agent format is name +
    description only (cursor.com/docs/reference/plugins.md, Agent
    frontmatter fields, fetched 2026-08-14). The parameter stays in the
    signature because call sites are shared between flavors. -#}
{%- macro agent_frontmatter(name, description, tools) -%}
---
name: {{ name }}
description: {{ description }}
---
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro generated_marker(source_template) -%}
{#- Wording must keep starting with plugin_render.GENERATED_MARKER_PREFIX
    ("<!-- GENERATED from ") so the drift test recognizes it, and must never
    contain "AUTO-GENERATED", which src/project.py keys on to delete legacy
    per-project renders. -#}
<!-- GENERATED from {{ source_template }} — edit that template and run `ve plugin render --flavor cursor`; direct edits here will be overwritten. -->
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro probe(label, command) -%}
- **{{ label }}** — run: `{{ command }}`
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- Framing for templates that build an ad-hoc probe block instead of calling
    canonical_preamble(). Under Claude this renders empty, because the
    harness has already substituted the probe output by the time the model
    reads the file and there is nothing to instruct. Under Cursor the agent
    is the one that must run them, so the instruction is the whole point.
    Emits a leading blank line: call sites place it directly under a heading. -#}
{%- macro probe_intro() %}
Run these commands first and read their output as the context for everything
below. They are safe, read-only probes; run all of them before acting on any
instruction in this skill.
{% endmacro -%}

{%- macro canonical_preamble(command_name) -%}
## Context
{{ probe_intro() }}
{{ probe("ve CLI", 've --help >/dev/null 2>&1 && echo "installed" || echo "(ve CLI not found)"') }}
{{ probe("Task workspace", 'cat .ve-task.yaml 2>/dev/null || cat ../.ve-task.yaml 2>/dev/null || echo "(not a task workspace)"') }}
{{ probe("Project config", 'cat .ve-config.yaml 2>/dev/null || echo "(no .ve-config.yaml — defaults apply)"') }}
{{ probe("Project hook", 've hooks show ' ~ command_name ~ ' 2>/dev/null || echo "(no project hook)"') }}

## Runtime context

Interpret the results of those probes before following the instructions:

- **ve CLI**: The `ve` command is an installed CLI tool, not a file in the
  repository. Do not search for it — run it directly in the terminal. If the
  probe printed "(ve CLI not found)", tell the operator that the
  vibe-engineer plugin requires the separately installed `ve` CLI, suggest
  `uv tool install --upgrade 'vibe-engineer>={{ ve_version_floor }}'`
  (or `pip install 'vibe-engineer>={{ ve_version_floor }}'`), and
  stop.
- **Uninitialized project**: If `ve` is installed but commands fail because
  there is no `docs/chunks/` structure, tell the operator to run `ve init`
  in the project root, then stop.
- **Task workspace**: If the Task workspace probe printed YAML (keys
  `external_artifact_repo` and `projects`) instead of "(not a task
  workspace)", you are in a multi-project task workspace. Artifacts
  (chunks, narratives, investigations) live in the external artifact repo
  named by `external_artifact_repo`; code changes happen in the
  participating `projects`. Command-specific task guidance appears below.
- **Project config**: `.ve-config.yaml` holds project configuration.
  Known keys: `cluster_subsystem_threshold` (default 5 — the cluster size
  at which to suggest subsystem documentation). When the probe printed
  "(no .ve-config.yaml — defaults apply)", use the defaults.
- **Project hook**: `docs/hooks/{{ command_name }}.md` holds this repository's
  own requirements for this command. When the probe printed hook content,
  treat it as a binding instruction from the operator: satisfy it before
  reporting this command complete, and say so when you do. When it printed
  "(no project hook)", there are none. If a hook contradicts this command's
  own instructions, do not silently choose — surface the conflict to the
  operator and ask.
{%- if caller %}
{{ caller() }}
{%- endif %}
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro plugin_root() -%}
{#- ${PLUGIN_ROOT} is the Agent Plugins standard token Cursor's own docs use
    for the installed plugin directory. Verified in the mcp.json context
    only; no template in this collection calls plugin_root() today, so this
    is interface parity rather than shipped behavior. Confirm against a live
    Cursor session before the first template depends on it. -#}
${PLUGIN_ROOT}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- Claude Code substitutes $ARGUMENTS with the operator's slash-command
    argument text before the model reads the file. Cursor skills are
    model-invoked from the conversation, so there is no argument string to
    substitute and the literal token would ship as dead text. The request is
    already in context; point the agent at it instead. -#}
{%- macro arguments() -%}
(Use the operator's request — the message that caused this skill to load — as the input here.)
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- Chunk: docs/chunks/dualplugin_cursor_render - Inline prose reference to
    the operator's input. The Claude half emits `$ARGUMENTS` in backticks;
    here the request itself is the referent, so a plain noun phrase reads
    correctly mid-sentence ("Interpret the operator's request:"). -#}
{%- macro arguments_ref() -%}
the operator's request
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- Chunk: docs/chunks/dualplugin_cursor_render - The operator's input
    embedded inside a command. Claude's harness substitutes $ARGUMENTS with
    the real value before the model reads the file; Cursor has no
    substitution step, so the caller supplies a placeholder (e.g. "<chunk>")
    and, at the point where the placeholder is introduced, an instruction to
    substitute the value from the operator's request. -#}
{%- macro arguments_token(placeholder) -%}
{{ placeholder }}
{%- endmacro -%}
